<|user|>
What is object-oriented programming?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Object-oriented programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that focuses on creating objects that contain both data and functions. The objects are defined by classes which act as blueprints for creating instances of the object. OOP is designed to make code more modular, reusable, and easier to understand and maintain. Some popular OOP languages include C++, Java, and Python. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do virtual functions work in C++?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Virtual functions in C++ allow a function to be overridden by a derived class. When a virtual function is called on an object of a base class, the implementation of the function to be executed is determined at runtime based on the type of the object being called. This allows for polymorphism, where different objects can be treated as if they were of the same class for the purpose of calling a function. To declare a virtual function in C++, the keyword 'virtual' is placed before the function declaration in the base class. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


