<|user|>
Given the following C++ code:
```c++
#include 
using namespace std;
int main() {
 int a = 7;
 int b = 2;
 int c = a * b;
 cout << "The value of a is: " << a << endl;
 cout << "The value of b is: " << b << endl;
 cout << "The value of c is: " << c << endl;
 return 0;
}
```
Using the same logic as the puzzle, can you modify the code to set the value of 'a' to the sum of its digits multiplied by 3 and set the value of 'b' to the product of its digits added to itself? Then print out the value of 'c'.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Certainly! Here's the modified code:
```c++
#include 
using namespace std;
int main() {
 int a = 7;
 int b = 2;
 int sum_of_digits = (a % 10) + (a / 10);
 int product_of_digits = (b % 10) * (b / 10) + b;
 a = sum_of_digits * 3;
 b = product_of_digits;
 int c = a * b;
 cout << "The value of a is: " << a << endl;
 cout << "The value of b is: " << b << endl;
 cout << "The value of c is: " << c << endl;
 return 0;
}
```
This code sets the value of 'a' to the sum of its digits multiplied by 3 and sets the value of 'b' to the product of its digits added to itself. Then, it calculates the value of 'c' as the product of 'a' and 'b' and prints out all three values. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
