How can I create a C# console application that reads data from a CSV file and displays it in an interactive table format? The table should be visually impressive and professional, and include the data's relevant details, such as achievements and key skills. Additionally, can you include a function that allows the user to sort the table based on a specific column?
[C# Code]
As for the sorting function, here it is:
```csharp
static void Main(string[] args)
{
 List people = ReadCSVFile("path/to/file.csv");
 //Sort by name
 people.Sort((x, y) => x.Name.CompareTo(y.Name));
 //Display table
 Console.WriteLine("NametAgetSkills");
 foreach(Person p in people)
 {
 Console.WriteLine("{0}t{1}t{2}", p.Name, p.Age, p.Skills);
 }
}
class Person
{
 public string Name { get; set; }
 public int Age { get; set; }
 public string Skills { get; set; }
 public Person(string name, int age, string skills)
 {
 Name = name;
 Age = age;
 Skills = skills;
 }
}
static List ReadCSVFile(string path)
{
 List people = new List();
 using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(path))
 {
 while (!reader.EndOfStream)
 {
 string[] values = reader.ReadLine().Split(',');
 string name = values[0];
 int age = int.Parse(values[1]);
 string skills = values[2];
 people.Add(new Person(name, age, skills));
 }
 }
 return people;
}
```
Additionally, can you include a function that randomly shuffles the table rows?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! Here's the modified code that includes the shuffling function:
```csharp
static void Main(string[] args)
{
 List people = ReadCSVFile("path/to/file.csv");
 //Sort by name
 people.Sort((x, y) => x.Name.CompareTo(y.Name));
 //Display table
 DisplayTable(people);
 //Shuffle rows
 Shuffle(people);
 //Display shuffled table
 DisplayTable(people);
}
class Person
{
 public string Name { get; set; }
 public int Age { get; set; }
 public string Skills { get; set; }
 public Person(string name, int age, string skills)
 {
 Name = name;
 Age = age;
 Skills = skills;
 }
}
static List ReadCSVFile(string path)
{
 List people = new List();
 using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(path))
 {
 while (!reader.EndOfStream)
 {
 string[] values = reader.ReadLine().Split(',');
 string name = values[0];
 int age = int.Parse(values[1]);
 string skills = values[2];
 people.Add(new Person(name, age, skills));
 }
 }
 return people;
}
static void DisplayTable(List people)
{
 Console.WriteLine("NametAgetSkills");
 foreach(Person p in people)
 {
 Console.WriteLine("{0}t{1}t{2}", p.Name, p.Age, p.Skills);
 }
}
static void Shuffle(List list)
{
 Random rng = new Random();
 int n = list.Count;
 while (n > 1)
 {
 n--;
 int k = rng.Next(n + 1);
 T value = list[k];
 list[k] = list[n];
 list[n] = value;
 }
}
```
The `Shuffle` function takes in a `List` where `T` can be any type of object, and shuffles the elements randomly using the Fisher-Yates algorithm. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain what the Fisher-Yates algorithm is and how it works?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! The Fisher-Yates algorithm, also known as the Knuth shuffle, is an algorithm for shuffling a finite sequence of elements. It was invented by Ronald Fisher and Frank Yates in 1938, and later popularized by Donald Knuth in his book "The Art of Computer Programming." The algorithm works by iterating through the elements of the sequence and swapping each element with a randomly-selected element that comes after it in the sequence.

Here's how it works:

1. Start at the last element of the sequence.
2. Generate a random number between 0 and the index of the current element (inclusive).
3. Swap the current element with the element at the randomly-generated index.
4. Move to the next-to-last element of the sequence and repeat steps 2-3.
5. Continue this process until you reach the first element of the sequence.

After this process, the sequence will be in random order. This algorithm has a time complexity of O(n), where n is the length of the sequence. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




