Can you explain the difference between a stack and a queue in data structures?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! Both stacks and queues are abstract data types used to store and organize data. The key difference between these two data structures is the way that items are added or removed from them.

A stack is a "last-in, first-out" (LIFO) data structure, which means that the most recently added item is the first one to be removed. Think of it like a stack of plates - you can only add or remove a plate from the top of the stack. This makes it useful for things like maintaining a history of actions in a program or evaluating expressions in reverse Polish notation.

In contrast, a queue is a "first-in, first-out" (FIFO) data structure, which operates more like a line at a grocery store. The first item added to the queue is the first one to be removed, and new items are added to the back of the queue. Queues are commonly used in scenarios where order matters, such as handling requests in a web server or processing jobs in a print queue.

