What is the difference between a hurricane and a tornado?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A hurricane is a large, rotating storm that forms over warm ocean waters and can spread over hundreds of miles. It has sustained winds of at least 74 mph and can cause significant damage to coastal areas. A tornado, on the other hand, is a rapidly rotating column of air that forms from thunderstorms and reaches the ground. It has winds that can exceed 200 mph and can cause extensive damage in a small area. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
