<|end|>
<|asst|>
In the theory of relativity, time dilation refers to the difference in the elapsed time measured by two observers, due to a relative velocity between them or to a difference in gravitational potential between their locations. As an object approaches the speed of light, time slows down for that object relative to a stationary observer. This effect has been verified experimentally and has important implications for our understanding of the nature of space and time. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
