<|fim_suf|>, unsupervised learning is a type of machine learning where the algorithm learns to associate patterns in data without being given an explicit output variable to predict. Essentially, the algorithm is provided with unlabeled data and is tasked with finding structure or hidden patterns within the data. Clustering is one popular type of unsupervised learning, where the algorithm groups similar data points together into clusters based on some similarity metric.

The key difference here is that in supervised learning, the algorithm is trained on labeled data, whereas in unsupervised learning, the algorithm must find patterns in unlabeled data on its own. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


