<|user|>
How can I modify this Ruby code to display a list of unique traditional Japanese snacks that are only found in specific regions or cities within Japan, along with the regions or cities where they are commonly found? 
Here's the Ruby code that can be used to generate the list:
snacks = {
 "Tokyo" => ["Monaka", "Kuzumochi", "Dorayaki"],
 "Kyoto" => ["Yatsuhashi", "Kyo Baum", "Arare"],
 "Hokkaido" => [<|fim_suf|>, "Marusei Butter Sand", "Soup Curry"],
 "Osaka" => ["Takoyaki", "Okonomiyaki", "Kushikatsu"],
 "Hiroshima" => ["Momiji Manju", "Oyster Pancake", "Hiroshima-style Okonomiyaki"]
}
snack_list = []
snacks.each do |region, list|
 list.each do |snack|
 if !snack_list.include?(snack)
 snack_list << snack
 puts "#{snack} is a traditional Japanese snack that is only found in #{region}."
 end
 end
end
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To modify the code to display a list of unique traditional Japanese snacks that are only found in specific regions or cities within Japan, you can update the code to iterate through each snack and its corresponding region, and then only display the snack if it is unique to that region. Here's the updated code:
```ruby
snacks = {
 "Tokyo" => ["Monaka", "Kuzumochi", "Dorayaki"],
 "Kyoto" => ["Yatsuhashi", "Kyo Baum", "Arare"],
 "Hokkaido" => ["Shiroi Koibito", "Marusei Butter Sand", "Soup Curry"],
 "Osaka" => ["Takoyaki", "Okonomiyaki", "Kushikatsu"],
 "Hiroshima" => ["Momiji Manju", "Oyster Pancake", "Hiroshima-style Okonomiyaki"]
}
snack_list = []
snacks.each do |region, list|
 list.each do |snack|
 if !snack_list.include?(snack)
 unique_snack = true
 snacks.each do |r, l|
 if r != region && l.include?(snack)
 unique_snack = false
 break
 end
 end
 if unique_snack
 snack_list << snack
 puts "#{snack} is a traditional Japanese snack that is only found in #{region}."
 end
 end
 end
end
```
This code will now only display snacks that are unique to a specific region or city within Japan. It does this by checking whether the snack appears in any other region's list of snacks, and only displaying it if it does not. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
