<|user|>
How can I use MATLAB to generate a sine wave with a frequency of 1 Hz and a sampling rate of 100 Hz? Can you also provide code to plot the waveform? Additionally, how can I modify the code to add noise to the signal with a signal-to-noise ratio of 20 dB? Finally, can you provide code to perform a Fourier analysis on the noisy signal and plot the frequency spectrum?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, here's some MATLAB code that can help you generate a sine wave with a frequency of 1 Hz and a sampling rate of 100 Hz:
```matlab
% Define the sampling rate and time vector
Fs = 100; % Sampling rate (Hz)
t = 0:1/Fs:1-1/Fs; % Time vector (seconds)
% Generate the sine wave
f = 1; % Frequency (Hz)
y = sin(2*pi*f*t); % Sine wave
% Plot the waveform
plot(t, y);
xlabel('Time (s)');
ylabel('Amplitude');
title('Sine Waveform');
```
To add noise to the signal with a signal-to-noise ratio of 20 dB, you can use the `awgn` function in MATLAB:
```matlab
% Add noise to the signal
SNR = 20; % Signal-to-noise ratio (dB)
y_noisy = awgn(y, SNR, 'measured');
% Plot the noisy waveform
plot(t, y_noisy);
xlabel('Time (s)');
ylabel('Amplitude');
title('Noisy Sine Waveform');
```
Finally, to perform a Fourier analysis on the noisy signal and plot the frequency spectrum, you can use the `fft` function in MATLAB:
```matlab
% Compute the Fourier transform of the noisy signal
Y = fft(y_noisy);
% Compute the frequency vector
f = Fs*(0:(length(y_noisy)-1))/length(y_noisy);
% Plot the frequency spectrum
plot(f, abs(Y));
xlabel('Frequency (Hz)');
ylabel('Magnitude');
title('Frequency Spectrum');
```
I hope this helps! Let me know if you have any further questions. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Thank you for the code! Can you explain what the `awgn` function does in more detail and how it adds noise to the signal?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! The `awgn` function in MATLAB stands for "Additive White Gaussian Noise". This function adds white noise, which is a type of random noise that has a constant power spectral density across all frequencies, to your signal. The amount of noise added is controlled by the second argument to the `awgn` function, which specifies the desired signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) in decibels (dB). The third argument to the `awgn` function specifies the method used to estimate the actual SNR of the noisy signal. In our case, we specified `'measured'`, which means that MATLAB will estimate the noise power based on the input signal. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


