<|fim_suf|>, while older investors who are closer to retirement should be more conservative with their investments. There are also various online tools and quizzes that can help you determine your risk tolerance. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between a mutual fund and an exchange-traded fund (ETF)?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Both mutual funds and ETFs are collective investment vehicles that allow individual investors to pool their money together to invest in a diversified portfolio of stocks, bonds, or other assets. However, there are some key differences between the two. Mutual funds are priced once a day after the market closes, while ETFs can be bought and sold throughout the trading day like any stock. Additionally, ETFs tend to have lower expense ratios than mutual funds because they are passively managed, which means they track an underlying index rather than being actively managed by a fund manager. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is dollar-cost averaging and how does it work?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Dollar-cost averaging is a strategy where you invest a fixed amount of money into an investment at regular intervals, regardless of the price of the investment. This means that when prices are high, you'll buy fewer shares, and when prices are low, you'll buy more shares. Over time, this strategy can help smooth out the volatility of the market, and you'll end up with a lower average cost per share than if you had invested a lump sum all at once. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


