The best time of day to exercise depends on a variety of factors, including personal preference, schedule, and the type of exercise being performed. Some people prefer to exercise early in the morning because it helps them start their day with an energy boost, while others prefer to work out in the evening as a way to relieve stress after a long day at work.

However, research suggests that there may be some advantages to exercising in the morning. A study published in the journal Sleep Medicine found that participants who exercised in the morning had significantly better sleep quality than those who exercised in the afternoon or evening. This is because exercise increases body temperature, which can interfere with sleep if done too close to bedtime. Exercising in the morning allows for the body temperature to return to normal by bedtime.

Another study published in the journal Obesity found that participants who exercised in the morning were more consistent with their workout routine than those who exercised later in the day. This may be because exercising in the morning requires less willpower than waiting until the end of the day when energy levels are lower and other obligations may interfere.

Overall, the best time of day to exercise is one that fits your schedule and allows you to be consistent with your workouts. <|endoftext|>
