<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between anaphora and cataphora, and provide examples for each?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Anaphora and cataphora are two types of reference in language. Anaphora refers to using a word or phrase to refer back to something that was previously mentioned, while cataphora is the use of a word or phrase to refer forward to something that will be mentioned later. 

An example of anaphora would be the sentence "John said he was tired." The pronoun "he" refers back to John, who was mentioned earlier. Another example of anaphora is "I went to the store, bought some apples, and ate them." The pronoun "them" refers back to "apples" that was mentioned earlier. 

An example of cataphora would be the sentence "When she arrived at the party, Mary couldn't believe it." The pronoun "it" refers forward to something that hasn't been mentioned yet. Another example of cataphora is "Before he could start cooking, John needed to buy some groceries." The subordinate clause "Before he could start cooking" refers forward to what John needs to do next.

In summary, anaphora refers back to something previously mentioned, while cataphora refers forward to something that will be mentioned later. Both types of reference play important roles in creating cohesion and coherence in language. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


