<|fim_suf|>, which means that the first item added to the queue is the first one to be removed. Think of it like a line of people waiting for a roller coaster. The person who gets in line first will be the first one to get on the ride, while the last person in line will have to wait until everyone else has gone.

In terms of implementation, both stacks and queues can be implemented using arrays or linked lists. For example, a stack can be implemented using an array with a variable top index, which points to the top element of the stack. When a new element is pushed onto the stack, the top index is incremented, and when an element is popped off the stack, the top index is decremented.

Similarly, a queue can be implemented using an array or a linked list. In an array-based implementation, we use a rear index to keep track of the last element in the queue, and a front index to keep track of the first element. When a new element is enqueued, it is added at the rear index, and when an element is dequeued, it is removed from the front index.

