Sure, I can help you with that. Here's how you can use conditional formatting in Excel to highlight cells that contain specific text:
1. Select the range of cells where you want to apply conditional formatting.
2. Click on the "Conditional Formatting" button in the "Styles" group on the "Home" tab.
3. Select "Highlight Cells Rules" from the drop-down menu, and then select "Text that Contains" from the sub-menu.
4. In the "Format Cells That Contain" dialog box, enter the text that you want to highlight, such as "Pending", in the "Value" field.
5. Choose the formatting options that you want to apply to the highlighted cells, such as the font color, fill color, and border style.
6. Click "OK" to apply the conditional formatting to the selected cells.

Once you've applied this conditional formatting rule, any cell in the selected range that contains the word "Pending" will be highlighted in the format that you specified. You can also use other text criteria to highlight cells that contain specific words or phrases, such as "Completed" or "In Progress".

