You need to create a Java program that categorizes an item as belonging to a particular group. The program should have a class called "Item" with the following properties: name (String), price (double), and category (String). The program should also have a class called "Group" with the following properties: name (String) and items (List).
Here is an example of how the program should work:
Item item1 = new Item("Ship", 1000000.0);
Item item2 = new Item("Car", 50000.0);
Item item3 = new Item("Bicycle", 1000.0);
Group group1 = new Group("Vehicles");
group1.addItem(item1);
group1.addItem(item2);
group1.addItem(item3);
Group group2 = new Group("Toys");
group2.addItem(item3);
System.out.println("Item: " + item1.getName() + " Category: " + item1.getCategory());
System.out.println("Item: " + item2.getName() + " Category: " + item2.getCategory());
System.out.println("Item: " + item3.getName() + " Category: " + item3.getCategory());
System.out.println("Group: " + group1.getName() + " Items: " + group1.getItems());
System.out.println("Group: " + group2.getName() + " Items: " + group2.getItems());
How can you modify the Item class to categorize the ship item as belonging to the "Vehicles" category?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To categorize the ship item as belonging to the "Vehicles" category, you can modify the Item class constructor to take in an additional parameter for category, like this:
```
public class Item {
 private String name;
 private double price;
 private String category;
 public Item(String name, double price, String category) {
 this.name = name;
 this.price = price;
 this.category = category;
 }
 // getters and setters for name, price, and category
}
```
Then, when you create the ship item, you can pass in "Vehicles" as the category:
```
Item item1 = new Item("Ship", 1000000.0, "Vehicles");
```
This will set the category of the ship item to "Vehicles". <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
