How can we use Python to plot points in a 3-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system? Can you provide a code example and explain how the x, y, and z coordinates are represented in this system? Additionally, how is this coordinate system utilized in fields such as physics, engineering, and computer graphics? Please refer to the accompanying diagram to support your explanation.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, we can use Python to plot points in a 3-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system using the Matplotlib library. Here is an example code:
```python
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
from mpl_toolkits.mplot3d import Axes3D
fig = plt.figure()
ax = fig.add_subplot(111, <|fim_suf|>, y, z)
ax.set_xlabel('X Label')
ax.set_ylabel('Y Label')
ax.set_zlabel('Z Label')
plt.show()
```
In this code, we first import the necessary libraries, then create a figure and a 3D subplot. We then define the x, y, and z coordinates of the points we want to plot and use the `scatter` method to plot them. Finally, we label the axes and show the plot.
In a 3-dimensional Cartesian coordinate system, each point is represented by an ordered triple (x, y, z), where x represents the horizontal axis, y represents the vertical axis, and z represents the depth axis. The axes are all perpendicular to each other and intersect at the origin (0, 0, 0).
This coordinate system is utilized in fields such as physics, engineering, and computer graphics to represent three-dimensional objects and phenomena. For example, in physics, the motion of objects in three-dimensional space can be represented using this coordinate system. In engineering, the design of three-dimensional objects can be visualized and analyzed using this system. In computer graphics, three-dimensional models can be created and rendered using this system. 
Here is a diagram to support the explanation:
![3D Cartesian Coordinate System](https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/3D_Cartesian_Coordinates.svg/1200px-3D_Cartesian_Coordinates.svg.png) <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
