<|user|>
What is the difference between supervised and unsupervised machine learning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The main difference between supervised and unsupervised machine learning is the presence or absence of labeled training data. In supervised learning, the training data includes input examples along with their corresponding output labels. The goal is to learn a function that maps inputs to outputs, given the labeled training data. This learned function can then be used to make predictions on new, unseen data. Examples of supervised learning algorithms include linear regression, decision trees, and neural networks.

In contrast, unsupervised learning involves finding patterns in the data without the use of labeled output data. The algorithm instead seeks to identify hidden structures or relationships within the data itself. Clustering, principal component analysis, and anomaly detection are examples of unsupervised learning techniques. Unsupervised learning is particularly useful when you don't have labeled data, or when there are too many dimensions in the data for humans to manually label each example.

To summarize, the key difference between supervised and unsupervised learning lies in whether the training data has labeled output information or not. Supervised learning uses labeled data to learn a mapping from inputs to outputs, while unsupervised learning finds patterns in the input data itself. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


