<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between discrete and continuous random variables?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, a discrete random variable is one that can only take on certain values, usually integers, such as the number of heads in a series of coin tosses or the number of cars sold in a day. A continuous random variable, on the other hand, can take on any value within a certain range, typically measured on a scale, such as time or weight. An example of a continuous random variable would be the height of a randomly chosen person, which could be any value within a certain range. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




