<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between subject and object pronouns?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Subject pronouns are used as the subject of a sentence, while object pronouns are used as the object of a verb or preposition. For example, "She is reading a book" uses the subject pronoun "she," while "I gave the book to her" uses the object pronoun "her." <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What are possessive pronouns?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Possessive pronouns are pronouns that show ownership or possession. Examples include "mine," "yours," "his," "hers," "its," "ours," and "theirs." They can be used to replace a noun phrase to show who possesses something, such as "That car is mine" instead of "That car belongs to me." <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you give an example of when reflexive pronouns are used?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Reflexive pronouns are used when the subject and the object of a sentence are the same. They typically end in "-self" or "-selves." For example, "He cut himself while shaving" or "They taught themselves how to play guitar." <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




