<|user|>
How does the concept of supply and demand affect pricing in an economy?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In a market economy, the price of a good or service is determined by the intersection of its supply and demand curves. When there is high demand for a product but limited supply, the price tends to rise because people are willing to pay more to obtain it. Conversely, when supply exceeds demand, the price tends to fall because sellers need to lower prices to find buyers. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the difference between a hypothesis and a theory?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A hypothesis is an educated guess or prediction about a phenomenon that can be tested through observation and experimentation. A theory, on the other hand, is a well-substantiated explanation of a set of related observations or phenomena that has been validated through repeated testing and experimentation. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do scientists determine the age of fossils?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
There are several methods that scientists use to determine the age of fossils, including radiometric dating, <|fim_suf|>, and stratigraphy. Radiometric dating involves measuring the decay of radioactive isotopes in rock layers surrounding fossils, while carbon dating measures the age of organic material such as bones or teeth. Stratigraphy involves analyzing the layer of rock in which a fossil is found to determine its relative age compared to other fossils and rock layers in the same geological formation. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


