<|user|>
What steps has Mexico taken in recent years to address the issue of migration?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Mexico has taken several steps in recent years to address the issue of migration. One of the most significant was the creation of the Mexican Commission for Refugee Assistance (COMAR) in 1990, which is responsible for providing protection and assistance to refugees and asylum seekers in Mexico. Additionally, Mexico has signed several international agreements on migration, including the Global Compact for Safe, Orderly and Regular Migration. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How has the COVID-19 pandemic impacted migration patterns in Mexico?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The COVID-19 pandemic has had a significant impact on migration patterns in Mexico. The closure of borders and travel restrictions have made it difficult for migrants to cross into the United States, leading many to stay in Mexico instead. At the same time, the economic downturn caused by the pandemic has made it harder for migrants to find work, exacerbating existing vulnerabilities. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Given these challenges, what measures has Mexico taken to support migrants during the pandemic?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Mexico has taken several measures to support migrants during the pandemic, including providing emergency financial assistance to vulnerable populations, including migrants. Additionally, the Mexican government has worked with civil society organizations to provide food, shelter, and medical services to migrants in need. However, these efforts have been hampered by limited resources and logistical challenges. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


