<|fim_suf|>, programmers have to manually allocate and deallocate memory, which can lead to memory leaks and other issues. Garbage collection automates this process by tracking which objects in memory are no longer needed by the program and reclaiming that memory. There are different algorithms for garbage collection, including mark-and-sweep and reference counting. Essentially, garbage collection runs periodically in the background and frees up any memory that is no longer being used by the program. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the difference between procedural and object-oriented programming?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Procedural programming is a programming paradigm that focuses on writing procedures or functions that perform specific tasks. It typically involves writing code that follows a sequence of instructions, with little focus on data structures or abstraction. Procedural programming languages include C and Pascal.

On the other hand, object-oriented programming (OOP) is a programming paradigm that emphasizes the use of objects, which are essentially data structures that contain both data and methods. The focus is on organizing code into reusable pieces, which can be used to build more complex systems. OOP languages include Java, Python, and Ruby.

The key difference between these two paradigms is their approach to organizing code. Procedural programming tends to be more focused on the algorithmic steps required to solve a problem, while OOP is more focused on organizing code into reusable objects that can interact with each other. OOP also offers features like inheritance and encapsulation, which are not available in procedural programming. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


