<|user|>
Can you provide an example of a C++ function that demonstrates the proverb "A stitch in time saves nine"?
Consider the following C++ code that performs a simple task of sorting an array:
void sortArray(int arr[], int n) {
 for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
 for (int j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
 if (arr[i] > arr[j]) {
 int temp = arr[i];
 arr[i] = arr[j];
 arr[j] = temp;
 }
 }
 }
}
However, we can optimize this code by adding a check to see if the array is already sorted before performing the sorting algorithm. This small change can save us a lot of time and resources in the long run.
Can you modify the code to demonstrate the proverb "A stitch in time saves nine" by adding this optimization?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Certainly! Here's the modified code with the optimization added:
void sortArray(int arr[], int n) {
 bool sorted = true;
 for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
 if (arr[i] > arr[i+1]) {
 sorted = false;
 break;
 }
 }
 if (!sorted) {
 for (int i = 0; i < n-1; i++) {
 for (int j = i+1; j < n; j++) {
 if (arr[i] > arr[j]) {
 int temp = arr[i];
 arr[i] = arr[j];
 arr[j] = temp;
 }
 }
 }
 }
}
This code first checks if the array is already sorted by iterating through the array and comparing adjacent elements. If it's already sorted, the function simply returns without performing any sorting. If it's not sorted, it performs the sorting algorithm as before. This small optimization can save a lot of time and resources in the long run, especially for large arrays that are already sorted. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
