The verb in the sentence is a modal verb "will". <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the use of modal verbs in English?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Modal verbs are auxiliary verbs that express modality, which refers to the speaker's attitude toward the action or state expressed by the main verb. Examples of modal verbs include "can," "could," "may," "might," "must," "shall," "should," "will," and "would." They are used to indicate possibility, permission, necessity, <|fim_suf|>, advice, obligation, and other related concepts. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you give me an example of how modals can be used to express possibility?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, one example is the sentence "It might rain today." The modal verb "might" expresses the possibility of rain occurring today. Other modal verbs that can be used to express possibility include "may," "could," and "can." <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What about expressing permission? Can you give me an example of that using a modal verb?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, an example would be the sentence "You may leave early if you finish your work." The modal verb "may" expresses permission for the person to leave early, but only if they complete their work. Another common modal verb used to express permission is "can." <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How about expressing obligation?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
An example of expressing obligation using a modal verb would be the sentence "You must study hard for the exam." The modal verb "must" indicates that studying hard for the exam is necessary, and there is no alternative. Other modal verbs that can be used to express obligation include "have to" and "should." <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


