The constraints ensure that the sides of the triangle are integers and that the sum of the lengths of any two sides is greater than the length of the remaining side. These conditions guarantee that the given set of points forms a valid triangle. There are other necessary conditions that a set of points must satisfy to form a triangle, such as having three distinct vertices and not being collinear. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
