Yes, the formula for finding the area of a triangle can be extended to three-dimensional space. In three dimensions, the area of a triangle is half the magnitude of the cross product of any two of its sides. 
The formula is:
```
Area = 1/2 * |(b-a) x (c-a)| = 1/2 * |(c-a) x (b-a)|
```
where `a`, `b`, and `c` are the position vectors of the three vertices of the triangle in three-dimensional space, and `x` represents the cross product. This formula works by finding the magnitude of the cross product of any two sides of the triangle and then dividing it by two to obtain the area of the triangle. <|endoftext|>
