<|fim_suf|>, an outer join is used to retrieve all rows from one table and matching rows from the other table(s). There are three types of outer joins: left outer join, right outer join, and full outer join. A left outer join returns all rows from the left table and matching rows from the right table(s), while a right outer join returns all rows from the right table and matching rows from the left table(s). A full outer join returns all rows from both tables.

Here's an example of a left outer join:

```
SELECT *
FROM table1
LEFT OUTER JOIN table2
ON table1.column = table2.column;
```

In this query, all rows from `table1` will be returned, even if there are no matching rows in `table2`. If there are matching rows in `table2`, they will be included in the result set as well.

