<|asst|>
There are a few ways to improve the efficiency of your code. One way is to use memoization to avoid recomputing values that have already been computed. You could create a `Map` to store the results of previous computations and look up values in the map before computing them again.

Another way to improve efficiency is to use tail recursion instead of regular recursion. This would eliminate the need for creating new stack frames for each recursive call, which can reduce memory usage and improve performance.

You could also consider using parallelism to take advantage of multiple processor cores. Scala provides several ways to achieve this, such as using the `par` method on collections to make them parallel or using the `Future` and `Promise` classes to execute tasks asynchronously.

Finally, you could optimize the `findNextWords` function by precomputing all possible monosyllabic words and storing them in a `Set`. This would eliminate the need for filtering out words that are already in the `words` list and improve the overall performance of the function. <|endoftext|>
