That's really helpful, thank you! I'm curious though -- how exactly do scientists measure genetic distance between different species?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Genetic distance is typically measured by comparing the DNA sequences of two species. The DNA sequences of two related species will be more similar than the DNA sequences of two distantly related species. Scientists can compare the DNA sequences of specific genes, or they can sequence the entire genome of each species and compare them. There are various algorithms and methods used to compare DNA sequences and calculate genetic distances, but the basic principle is that the more similar the DNA sequences, the smaller the genetic distance between the two species. <|endoftext|>
