<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! Here's the C++ code to determine the closest relative of the fox based on genetic distance:
```c++
#include 
int main() {
 double fox_wolf = 0.2;
 double fox_cat = 0.5;
 double fox_dog = 0.3;
 double fox_raccoon = 0.6;
 std::string closest_relative;
 double smallest_distance = fox_wolf;
 if (fox_cat < smallest_distance) {
 smallest_distance = fox_cat;
 closest_relative = "cat";
 }
 if (fox_dog < smallest_distance) {
 smallest_distance = fox_dog;
 closest_relative = "dog";
 }
 if (fox_raccoon < smallest_distance) {
 smallest_distance = fox_raccoon;
 closest_relative = "raccoon";
 }
 std::cout << "The closest relative of the fox is the " << closest_relative << "." << std::endl;
 return 0;
}
```
This code initializes the genetic distances between the fox and each animal, and then uses a series of if-else statements to compare the distances and determine the closest relative. The closest relative is stored in the `closest_relative` string variable, <|fim_mid|>and the name of the closest relative is outputted to the console. In this case<|fim_eom|>

