<|fim_suf|>, dark matter does not interact electromagnetically, making it invisible to telescopes and other instruments that detect light. Dark matter can only be detected through its gravitational effects on visible matter.

The existence of dark matter is inferred from observations of the motions of galaxies and galaxy clusters. The observed motions suggest that there is more mass present than can be accounted for by visible matter alone. The gravitational effects of this unseen mass are felt by the visible matter, resulting in the characteristic shapes and motions of galaxies and clusters.

The importance of dark matter lies in its role in the structure and evolution of the universe. Without dark matter, it is believed that galaxies would not have formed as they have, and the large-scale structure of the universe would look very different. Dark matter also plays a key role in the formation of stars and the evolution of galaxies, as it provides the gravitational force necessary to draw gas and dust together to form new stars.

Despite its importance, the nature of dark matter remains one of the greatest mysteries facing astrophysics today. There are several theories about what dark matter might be made of, but so far, none of them have been confirmed through direct detection experiments. Scientists continue to search for clues that could help unravel the mystery of dark matter and its role in the universe. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


