Here is an example of a Petrarchan sonnet with the ABBAABBA CDCDCD rhyme scheme and iambic pentameter that explores the theme of unrequited love:
```
xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?

Unrequited Love
John Smith

My heart is filled with longing for her love
But she remains indifferent to my pain
Her eyes are cold, her heart as hard as stone
Yet still I hope that she will feel the same
I dream of holding her in my embrace
Of kissing her sweet lips and feeling whole
But she is distant, and my heart's apace
With pain and longing for her to console
The days go by, my love remains the same
But she is gone, and I am left alone
Time cannot heal the wounds of love's cruel game
My heart is broken, and my spirit flown
Yet still I hope, though hope is all in vain
```
In this sonnet, the first eight lines (the octave) express the speaker's longing for the love of another person who remains indifferent to their feelings. The sestet (the last six lines) explore the theme of unrequited love further, with the speaker lamenting the passage of time and the fact that their love remains unreturned. <|endoftext|>
