<|user|>
What would be the LaTeX code to update a specific formula in a document with multiple equations? The equation to be updated is:
begin{equation}
x^2 + y^2 = z^2
end{equation}
and it should be changed to:
begin{equation}
x^2 + y^2 = r^2
end{equation}
Please provide the specific LaTeX code for this update.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To update the equation from $z^2$ to $r^2$, you can simply replace the letter "z" with "r" in the equation. Here is the updated LaTeX code:
begin{equation}
x^2 + y^2 = r^2
end{equation}
If you want to update the equation number as well, you can use the tag{} command. Here's an example:
begin{equation}
x^2 + y^2 = z^2 tag{1}
end{equation}
After updating the equation, you can use the tag{} command again to change the equation number:
begin{equation}
x^2 + y^2 = r^2 tag{1'}
end{equation}
This will change the equation number from (1) to (1'). <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What would be the LaTeX code to highlight a specific word in a document?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To highlight a specific word in a LaTeX document, you can use the colorbox command. Here's an example:

colorbox{yellow}{highlighted word}

This will create a yellow background behind the text "highlighted word". You can also change the color by replacing "yellow" with another color name or hex code. 

If you want to define your own highlighting style, you can use the newcommand command to create a custom macro. Here's an example:

newcommand{myhighlight}[1]{colorbox{pink}{#1}}

You can then use the myhighlight command to highlight any text you want:

myhighlight{highlighted word}

This will create a pink background behind the text "highlighted word". <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




