A combination, on the other hand, is a selection of objects without regard to the order. For example, if we have three objects A, B, and C, there are three combinations of these objects taken two at a time: AB, AC, and BC. The formula for calculating the number of combinations of n objects taken r at a time is n!/r!(n-r)!.

