Can you explain the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning in machine learning?
Supervised learning is a type of machine learning where an algorithm learns from labeled data, meaning that the input data has already been categorized or classified for it. Unsupervised learning, on the other hand, involves using unlabeled data to identify patterns or relationships within the data itself without any predefined categories or labels. Would you like more information on this topic?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! Supervised learning is commonly used for tasks such as image classification or language translation where the algorithm needs to learn from a large dataset of pre-labeled examples to accurately recognize patterns in new, <|fim_mid|>unseen data. In contrast<|fim_eom|>

