Can you explain the difference between supervised and unsupervised learning in machine learning?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! In supervised learning, we train a model using labeled data, which means that each input has a corresponding output. The goal is for the model to learn the relationship between inputs and outputs so it can make accurate predictions on new, unseen data. On the other hand, in unsupervised learning, we do not have labeled data, and the model must find patterns and structure on its own. Clustering is a common example of unsupervised learning where the goal is to group similar objects together based on their features. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


