<|user|>
How can I modify this Swift code block to print only even numbers in the range from 0 to 10? 
```
for number in 0...10 {
 if number % 2 == 0 {
 print("Hello World!")
 }
}
```
Please provide a line of code that can be added to this block to achieve this modification, and explain how this line of code utilizes conditional statements to filter the output properly.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To modify the code block to print only even numbers in the range from 0 to 10, you can change the print statement to print the number variable instead of the string "Hello World!". Here's the modified code block:
```
for number in 0...10 {
 if number % 2 == 0 {
 print(number)
 }
}
```
This code block uses a conditional statement (`if number % 2 == 0`) to check if the current value of `number` is even. If the remainder of `number` divided by 2 is equal to 0, then `number` is even and the code inside the `if` block is executed. In this case, we're just printing the value of `number` to the console. If the remainder of `number` divided by 2 is not equal to 0 (i.e. `number` is odd), then the code inside the `if` block is not executed, and the loop moves on to the next value of `number`. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between a stack and a queue data structure, and give an example of when to use each?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, a stack is a data structure that follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle, meaning that the element added last will be removed first. A queue, on the other hand, <|fim_suf|>, meaning that the element added first will be removed first.

An example of when to use a stack would be when implementing undo/redo functionality in a text editor. Each time a user performs an action, such as typing a character or deleting a word, we can push that action onto the stack. If the user wants to undo their last action, we can simply pop the top action off the stack to reverse it. Similarly, if the user wants to redo an action they previously undid, we can push that action back onto the stack.

An example of when to use a queue would be when processing tasks in a first-come-first-served manner. For instance, if we have multiple users submitting requests to a server, we can add those requests to a queue and process them one-by-one in the order they were received. This ensures that each request receives equal priority and is processed fairly in the order it was submitted. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


