How can I use C++ to read a text file and highlight all occurrences of a specific word with a different color? Can you provide an example code?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, I can help you with that. Here is an example code that reads a text file named "input.txt" and highlights all occurrences of the word "example" with the color red:
```
#include 
#include 
#include 
using namespace std;
int main() {
 string filename = "input.txt";
 string word = "example";
 string line;
 ifstream file(filename);
 if (file.is_open()) {
 while (getline(file, line)) {
 size_t pos = 0;
 while ((pos = line.find(word, pos)) != string::npos) {
 line.insert(pos, "033[31m");
 pos += 7;
 line.insert(pos, "033[0m");
 pos += 4;
 }
 cout << line << endl;
 }
 file.close();
 }
 return 0;
}
```
In this code, we first define the filename and the word we want to highlight. We then open the file using `ifstream` and loop through each line of the file using `getline`. For each line, we search for all occurrences of the word using the `find` function. If we find an occurrence, we insert the ANSI escape codes for red color (`033[31m` and `033[0m`) before and after the word, respectively. We then print the modified line to the console.
Note that this code assumes that your terminal supports ANSI escape codes for color. If your terminal does not support them, the highlighted text will appear as gibberish. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
