<|user|>
I have a list of temperature readings in Fahrenheit stored in an Excel table as shown below:
| Temperature (°F) |
|:---------------------:|
| 78 |
| 92 |
| 104 |
| 68 |
How do I convert these Fahrenheit temperatures to Celsius using a formula in Excel? Can you also output the converted temperatures in Celsius in a new column next to the Fahrenheit temperatures in the same table?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, I can help you with that. 
To convert Fahrenheit temperatures to Celsius in Excel, you can use the following formula:
=(Fahrenheit temperature - 32) * 5/9
To output the converted temperatures in Celsius in a new column next to the Fahrenheit temperatures, you can follow these steps:
1. Insert a new column next to the Temperature (°F) column.
2. Enter the header "Temperature (°C)" in the new column.
3. In the first cell of the new column (cell B2), enter the formula:
=(A2-32)*5/9
4. Copy the formula down to the rest of the cells in the new column by dragging the fill handle (the small square at the bottom right corner of the cell) down to the last row of the table.
The resulting table should look like this:
| Temperature (°F) | Temperature (°C) |
|:---------------------:|:--------------------:|
| 78 | 25.56 |
| 92 | 33.33 |
| 104 | 40.00 |
| 68 | 20.00 | <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
