<|user|>
Can you explain the concept of dark matter and why it is important in astronomy?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Dark matter refers to a hypothetical form of matter that does not interact with light or other forms of electromagnetic radiation, making it invisible to telescopes. It is believed to make up approximately 85% of the total mass of the universe. The existence of dark matter was first proposed as a way to account for discrepancies between observed gravitational effects and the amount of visible matter in galaxies and galaxy clusters.

The importance of dark matter in astronomy lies in its role in shaping the large-scale structure of the universe. Without dark matter, the gravity of visible matter would not be sufficient to hold galaxies and clusters together, and they would have dispersed long ago. In addition, dark matter provides a useful tool for studying the distribution of mass in the universe, as its gravitational effects can be observed indirectly through its influence on visible matter. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


