<|asst|>
Sure, compound interest is the interest that is earned not only on the initial amount invested or borrowed, but also on any interest that has accumulated over time. In other words, it's interest on interest. It can be a powerful tool for growing your savings or investments over time, as it allows you to earn interest on your principal as well as on the interest you've already earned. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you give me an example of how compound interest works?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure. Let's say you invest $1,000 in an account that pays 5% interest per year, compounded annually. After the first year, you will have earned $50 in interest, bringing your total balance to $1,050. In the second year, you will earn 5% interest on $1,050, which is $52.50. So after two years, your total balance will be $1,102.50. As you can see, the interest earned in the first year was added to the principal, so in the second year you earned interest on both the original principal and the interest from the first year. This compounding effect can really add up over time. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Is there a formula for calculating compound interest?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, the formula for calculating compound interest is:
A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt)
Where:
A = the final amount
P = the principal (initial amount invested or borrowed)
r = the annual interest rate (expressed as a decimal)
n = the number of times the interest is compounded per year
t = the number of years

For example, if you were to invest $10,000 at an annual interest rate of 6%, compounded monthly for 5 years, the formula would look like this:
A = 10,<|fim_suf|>,382.05
So after 5 years, your investment would be worth $13,382.05, assuming you didn't make any additional deposits or withdrawals during that time. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


