Can you explain the difference between an abstract class and an interface in Java?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, an abstract class can have concrete methods with implementation while an interface cannot have any concrete method implementations. In Java, a class can implement multiple Interfaces but can extend only one Abstract class. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How does the Garbage Collector work in Java?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Garbage Collector is responsible for freeing up memory that is no longer being used by an application. It automatically identifies and deletes objects that are no longer needed by the program. The JVM allocates memory to objects as and when required, and when the objects are no longer referenced, the Garbage Collector marks them for deletion. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the difference between a stack and a queue in Java?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
A Stack follows the Last-In-First-Out (LIFO) principle, which means that the last element inserted into the stack is the first element to be removed. A Queue follows the First-In-First-Out (FIFO) principle, which means that the first element inserted into the queue is the first element to be removed. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
What is the purpose of the "this" keyword in Java?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The "this" keyword in Java refers to the current object instance on which the method is invoked. It can be used to refer to the current object's attributes or methods within a class. It is useful when there is a name conflict between instance variables and parameters in a constructor or method. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain the difference between checked and unchecked exceptions in Java?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Checked exceptions are those that are checked at compile-time and must be handled by the developer. Examples include IOException, SQLException, and ClassNotFoundException. Unchecked exceptions, on the other hand, are not checked at compile-time and do not need to be handled explicitly. Examples include NullPointerException, ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException, and ClassCastException. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




