<|fim_suf|>, it is the distance from the center of the polygon to the midpoint of one of its sides. The apothem is useful for calculating the area of a regular polygon because it bisects each side of the polygon and forms right triangles with half of each side and the apothem as the hypotenuse. The area of each triangle can be calculated as (1/2)bh, where b is half of a side length and h is the apothem length. Therefore, the total area of the polygon can be calculated as the sum of the areas of all the triangles. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
That makes sense. How would you calculate the perimeter of the polygon given its side lengths?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To calculate the perimeter of a polygon, you simply need to add up the lengths of all its sides. For a regular polygon like the one we have been discussing, where all sides have the same length, you can calculate the perimeter by multiplying the number of sides by the length of one side. So for a 5-sided polygon where each side is a multiple of 7, the perimeter would be 5 times 7, which is 35. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


