<|user|>
Write a JavaScript function for changing the background color of a website.
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Here is a simple JavaScript function that changes the background color of a website when called:

```
function changeBackgroundColor(color) {
 document.body.style.backgroundColor = color;
}
```

To use this function, simply call it with the color you want as the parameter:

```
changeBackgroundColor('red');
```

This will change the background color of the website to red. You can use any valid CSS color value as the parameter, such as "blue", "#FFAABB", or "rgb(255, 30, 120)". <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How can I add an event listener to a button in JavaScript?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To add an event listener to a button in JavaScript, you can use the `addEventListener` method. Here is an example code snippet that shows how to add a click event listener to a button with the id "myButton":

```
const myButton = document.getElementById('myButton');

myButton.addEventListener('click', () => {
 console.log('Button clicked!');
});
```

This code creates a variable called `myButton` that references the button element with the id "myButton". It then adds a click event listener to the button using the `addEventListener` method. When the button is clicked, the function passed as the second argument to `addEventListener` is executed.

In this case, the function simply logs a message to the console. You can replace this function with any other function that you want to be executed when the button is clicked. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


