In scientific research, inductive reasoning is often used to develop hypotheses about how the natural world works. Scientists observe a phenomenon and then form a hypothesis about why that phenomenon occurs based on their observations. They then perform experiments or collect more data to test their hypothesis, refining it as they go along. <|endoftext|>
