<|user|>
What is the difference between a left join and a right join in SQL?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
In SQL, a left join and a right join are both types of outer joins. The main difference between them is the way they handle non-matching rows between the tables being joined. 

A left join returns all the rows from the "left" table (the one specified before the "LEFT JOIN" keyword) and the matching rows from the "right" table (specified after the "JOIN" keyword). If there is no match in the "right" table, the result will contain NULL values for the columns of that table.

A right join, on the other hand, returns all the rows from the "right" table and the matching rows from the "left" table. If there is no match in the "left" table, the result will contain NULL values for the columns of that table.

In general, the choice between a left join and a right join depends on the specific requirements of your query and the structure of your data. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


