<|user|>
Can you explain the Goldbach conjecture?
noinput
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Goldbach conjecture is a famous unsolved problem in number theory that states that every even integer greater than 2 can be expressed as the sum of two prime numbers. For example, 4 can be expressed as 2+2, 6 as 3+3, 8 as 3+5, 10 as 3+7 or 5+5, and so on. Although the conjecture has been verified for vast range of integers, no one has been able to prove it for all even numbers. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Why is it difficult to prove the Riemann hypothesis?
noinput
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The Riemann hypothesis is a famous conjecture in mathematics that proposes a formula to predict the distribution of prime numbers. It has been a longstanding and important open problem because it would help us understand fundamental aspects of number theory. It is difficult to prove the Riemann hypothesis because it requires complex mathematical techniques including complex analysis, topology, and geometry. Additionally, the conjecture is closely linked with the distribution of primes which is an elusive and poorly understood topic. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>




