<|end|>
<|asst|>
Apex predators such as wolves play a critical role in maintaining the balance of their ecosystems. They help regulate herbivore populations by preying on them, which in turn affects the abundance and distribution of plant species. Wolves also act as keystone species, meaning that their presence has a significant impact on the structure and function of their ecosystem. For example, studies have shown that the reintroduction of wolves to Yellowstone National Park in the 1990s had far-reaching effects on the park's ecosystem, including changes to the behavior of elk and other prey species, as well as increases in the abundance of vegetation and other wildlife. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How do wolves hunt?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Wolves are highly skilled hunters with a number of adaptations that make them effective predators. They typically hunt in packs, using coordinated tactics to take down prey that is often much larger than any individual wolf could handle alone. Their hunting strategy usually involves stalking and chasing their prey over long distances until it becomes exhausted, <|fim_mid|>at which point the wolves will move in for the kill. Wolves have also been known to employ a variety of other hunting techniques<|fim_eom|>

