<|user|>
Can I use conditional formatting in Excel to highlight the rows where the quantity of a particular fruit is less than a certain amount?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use conditional formatting in Excel to highlight the rows where the quantity of a particular fruit is less than a certain amount. Here are the steps:
1. Select the entire table.
2. Click on "Conditional Formatting" in the "Styles" group on the Home tab of the ribbon.
3. Select "New Rule".
4. In the "New Formatting Rule" dialog box, select "Use a formula to determine which cells to format".
5. In the formula box, enter the formula that will check for the condition you want. For example, if you want to highlight the rows where the quantity of apples is less than 10, you can enter the formula "=B2<10" (assuming the quantity column is column B and the first row of data is row 2).
6. Click on the "Format..." button and choose the formatting options you want for the highlighted rows.
7. Click "OK" to close the "New Formatting Rule" dialog box.
8. Click "OK" again to close the "Conditional Formatting Rules Manager" dialog box.

Now, the rows that meet the specified condition will be highlighted according to the formatting options you selected. You can adjust the formula to highlight rows based on other conditions as well. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


