<|fim_suf|>, unseen data. Examples of supervised learning algorithms include regression and classification.

In contrast, unsupervised learning does not use labeled data. Instead, the algorithm is given unstructured or unlabeled data and must derive structure or patterns from the data without knowing the correct output. Examples of unsupervised learning algorithms include clustering and dimensionality reduction.

The main difference between supervised and unsupervised learning is that supervised learning requires labeled data for training, while unsupervised learning does not. Supervised learning is typically used when we have a clear idea of what we want to predict, while unsupervised learning is used when we want to discover hidden patterns or structure in the data. <|endoftext|>
