Compose a sonnet that follows the traditional 14-line structure, with the first four lines serving as an introduction, the next four lines presenting a problem or conflict, and the final six lines providing a resolution or conclusion. Additionally, the poem should include an ABAB CDCD EFEF GG rhyme scheme and follow iambic pentameter, with each line consisting of ten syllables and a pattern of unstressed and stressed syllables. Use sensory language to vividly describe the setting and characters, and incorporate metaphors or similes to add depth and complexity to the poem. Finally, include at least one allusion to a famous work of literature or historical event to deepen the meaning and resonance of the poem.
