<|user|>
Can you explain how the brain processes language?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Language processing in the brain involves many different areas and neural networks. When we hear or read words, they are first processed in the auditory or visual cortex. From there, the information is sent to the Wernicke's area, which helps us understand the meaning of the words. Next, the information is sent to Broca's area, which is responsible for producing speech or writing. Finally, the motor cortex sends signals to our muscles to produce the physical movements required for speech or writing. This process is highly complex and involves many different regions of the brain working together. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


