<|fim_suf|>, where the input and output pairs are already known. The goal is to learn a function that can accurately predict the output for new inputs based on this training data. Some examples of supervised learning include regression, classification, and object detection.

In contrast, unsupervised learning involves learning from unlabeled data, where the algorithm tries to identify patterns or structures in the data without any pre-existing labels. This can involve tasks such as clustering or dimensionality reduction. An example of unsupervised learning is k-means clustering, where the algorithm tries to group similar data points together based on their features.

