Let me break it down into steps:

1. We start with the outer loop that goes from 0 to n-1. This loop will run n times, where n is the length of the list.
2. Inside the outer loop, we have another loop that goes from 0 to n-i-1. Since the last i elements are already sorted, we don't need to compare them again. Hence, we can reduce the number of comparisons in each iteration.
3. In the inner loop, we compare adjacent elements and swap them if they are in the wrong order. So, if the price of the jth item is greater than the price of the (j+1)th item, we swap them.
4. We keep doing this until no more swaps are needed. At this point, the list is sorted in ascending order based on the "price" key.

