<|user|>
Can you explain what the box model is in CSS?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
The box model in CSS is a way to describe how elements on a webpage are sized and laid out. It consists of four parts: the content area, padding, border, and margin. The content area contains the actual content of the element, while padding adds space between the content and the border. The border surrounds the padding and content, and the margin adds space between the border and other elements on the page. Understanding the box model is important for creating layouts that look consistent across different browsers and devices. <|endoftext|>
