<|user|>
Okay, that makes sense. Can you give an example of a situation where using a cell array would be more appropriate than using a regular array?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure! One common use case for cell arrays is when you are dealing with tables of data that have varying numbers of columns or rows. For example, suppose you have a dataset where each row represents a person and contains information such as their name, age, occupation, and income. Some people might have additional information available, such as hobbies or education level, while others might not. Rather than trying to store this information in a rectangular matrix with fixed dimensions, you could use a cell array where each row corresponds to a person and each column corresponds to a different piece of information. This way, you can easily add or remove columns as needed without having to resize the entire array. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


