<|fim_suf|>, int right) {
 if (left < right) {
 int mid = (left + right) / 2;
 mergeSort(arr, left, mid);
 mergeSort(arr, mid + 1, right);
 merge(arr, left, mid, right);
 }
 }
 
 public static void merge(int[] arr, int left, int mid, int right) {
 int[] temp = new int[right - left + 1];
 int i = left, j = mid + 1, k = 0;
 while (i <= mid && j <= right) {
 if (arr[i] <= arr[j]) {
 temp[k++] = arr[i++];
 } else {
 temp[k++] = arr[j++];
 }
 }
 while (i <= mid) {
 temp[k++] = arr[i++];
 }
 while (j <= right) {
 temp[k++] = arr[j++];
 }
 for (int x = 0; x < temp.length; x++) {
 arr[left + x] = temp[x];
 }
 }
 
 public static void main(String[] args) {
 int[] arr = {5, 2, 8, 3, 1, 6, 9, 7, 4};
 mergeSort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1);
 for (int i : arr) {
 System.out.print(i + " ");
 }
 }
}
```
Time complexity: O(n log n) - This is because the merge sort algorithm divides the array into two halves recursively until the base case is reached. At each level, the algorithm performs n operations (merging two sorted arrays) and the number of levels is log n. Therefore, the time complexity is O(n log n).
Space complexity: O(n) - This is because the merge sort algorithm creates a temporary array of size n to store the sorted elements during the merging process. The space complexity is proportional to the size of the input array. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can you explain what Big O notation is and how it's used to describe time and space complexity in algorithms?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Sure, Big O notation is a mathematical notation used to describe the behavior of functions as their input approaches infinity. It is commonly used in computer science to analyze the performance of algorithms.

When analyzing an algorithm's time complexity, we use Big O notation to express the upper bound of the number of operations an algorithm performs as a function of the size of its input. For example, if an algorithm takes n^2 operations to complete on an input of size n, we say that the algorithm has a worst-case time complexity of O(n^2).

Similarly, when analyzing an algorithm's space complexity, we use Big O notation to express the upper bound of the amount of memory an algorithm uses as a function of the size of its input. For example, if an algorithm uses an array of size n to store its output, we say that the algorithm has a worst-case space complexity of O(n).

Big O notation allows us to compare the performance of different algorithms and choose the most efficient one for a given problem. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


