<|user|>
How do black holes form?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Black holes are formed from the gravitational collapse of massive stars at the end of their lives. When a star exhausts its fuel and can no longer sustain nuclear fusion in its core, it begins to collapse under its own weight due to gravity. If the star is massive enough, this collapse becomes runaway, eventually causing the star's core to collapse into a single point known as a singularity. The gravitational pull of this singularity is so strong that nothing, not even light, can escape its event horizon, creating a region of space-time from which nothing can escape - a black hole. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


