<|fim_suf|>, on the other hand, is the process by which a plant increases in girth or thickness. This occurs due to the activity of lateral meristems, specifically the vascular cambium and cork cambium. The vascular cambium produces new xylem and phloem cells, which are responsible for conducting water and nutrients throughout the plant. The cork cambium, meanwhile, produces cork cells, which form a protective layer around the stem.

In terms of cell division and tissue development, primary growth involves cell division in the apical meristem, which gives rise to the three primary meristems mentioned earlier. Secondary growth involves cell division in the lateral meristem, which gives rise to secondary xylem and phloem cells as well as cork cells. While primary growth primarily contributes to the length and height of the plant, secondary growth contributes to its girth or thickness. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


