<|fim_suf|>, which is obtained by subtracting the integer part from the original value. Multiplying by this large number effectively moves the decimal point 10 places to the right, so that we can extract the repeating pattern of digits as a string and use it to construct the fraction. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
That makes sense. What if I want to convert the fractions back to decimals using the same repeating pattern? Is there an SQL query for that?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use a similar approach to convert the fractions back to decimals using the same repeating pattern. Here's the SQL code:
```
SELECT fraction, CAST(
 CONCAT(
 REPLACE(SUBSTRING_INDEX(fraction, '.', 1), ',', ''),
 SUBSTRING(SUBSTRING_INDEX(fraction, '.', -1), 1, 10),
 '(',
 REPEAT('9', LENGTH(SUBSTRING(SUBSTRING_INDEX(fraction, '.', -1), 2))),
 ')'
 ) AS DECIMAL(20, 10)
) AS decimal_value
FROM (
 SELECT CONCAT(
 FLOOR(value),
 '.',
 SUBSTRING(
 CAST(
 CAST(value - FLOOR(value) AS DECIMAL(10, 10)) * POW(10, 10) 
 AS UNSIGNED
 )
 AS CHAR
 ),
 REPEAT(
 SUBSTRING(
 CAST(
 CAST(value - FLOOR(value) AS DECIMAL(10, 10)) * POW(10, 10)
 AS UNSIGNED
 )
 AS CHAR
 ),
 50
 )
 ) AS fraction
 FROM decimals
) AS t;
```
This SQL query uses the `REPLACE()` function to remove any commas from the integer part of the fraction (since some countries use commas instead of periods as decimal separators). It then concatenates the integer and fractional parts of the fraction with a repeating pattern enclosed in parentheses. The repeating pattern is constructed by repeating the digit `9` for the length of the non-repeating part of the fractional component. Finally, it casts the resulting string as a decimal value with 20 digits of precision and 10 decimal places. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


