<|fim_suf|>, how can I display the image from the following Unsplash URL and add a caption with the photo's author and the theme described in one sentence: https://unsplash.com/photos/3E_a4eteVMs ?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To display the image from the Unsplash URL and add a caption with the photo's author and theme, you can use the following HTML code:
```

![Photo by Erik Mclean on Unsplash](https://unsplash.com/photos/3E_a4eteVMs)
Photo by Erik Mclean on Unsplash. A close-up of a green leafy plant with water droplets.

```
This code creates a `figure` element that contains an `img` element with the `src` attribute set to the Unsplash URL. The `alt` attribute provides alternative text for the image in case it cannot be displayed. The `figcaption` element contains the caption with the photo's author and theme. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How can I use CSS to make the caption text italic and align it to the center?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To make the caption text italic and align it to the center, you can use CSS. Here's the updated code:
```

![Photo by Erik Mclean on Unsplash](https://unsplash.com/photos/3E_a4eteVMs)
Photo by Erik Mclean on Unsplash. A close-up of a green leafy plant with water droplets.

```
The `style` attribute in the `figcaption` element allows you to include CSS directly in the HTML code. The `font-style` property sets the text to italic, while the `text-align` property centers the text within its container. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
How can I make the image responsive so that it adjusts its size based on the screen size?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
To make the image responsive, you can use CSS. Here's the updated code:
```

 figure {
 display: block;
 margin: 0;
 max-width: 100%;
 height: auto;
 }
 
 img {
 display: block;
 width: 100%;
 height: auto;
 }

 figcaption {
 font-style: italic; 
 text-align: center;
 }
![Photo by Erik Mclean on Unsplash](https://unsplash.com/photos/3E_a4eteVMs)
Photo by Erik Mclean on Unsplash. A close-up of a green leafy plant with water droplets.

```
This code adds a `style` section that contains CSS rules for the `figure`, `img`, and `figcaption` elements. The `max-width: 100%;` property in the `figure` rule sets the maximum width of the image to be equal to the width of its container, and the `height: auto;` property makes sure that the image's height adjusts proportionally. The `width: 100%;` property in the `img` rule sets the width of the image to be equal to the width of its container, and the `height: auto;` property makes sure that the image's height adjusts proportionally. This ensures that the image will adjust its size based on the screen size. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>
<|user|>
Can I use this same approach to make videos responsive as well?
<|end|>
<|asst|>
Yes, you can use a similar approach to make videos responsive. Here's an example code for an embedded YouTube video:
```

 .video-container {
 position: relative;
 padding-bottom: 56.25%;
 padding-top: 30px;
 height: 0;
 overflow: hidden;
 }
 
 .video-container iframe,
 .video-container object,
 .video-container embed {
 position: absolute;
 top: 0;
 left: 0;
 width: 100%;
 height: 100%;
 }

```
This code uses a container with a responsive aspect ratio (set by the `padding-bottom` property) to wrap the video. The `position: absolute;` rule in the video element's CSS positions it within the container, and the `width: 100%;` and `height: 100%;` rules make it fill the container. You can adjust the `padding-bottom` value to change the aspect ratio of the video. <|endoftext|>
<|end|>


